1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,300
Every political ad: ominous music, black-and-white slow motion, a grainy photo, RED ALL-CAPS TEXT.

2
00:00:03,300 --> 00:00:04,400
Let's translate.

3
00:00:04,400 --> 00:00:08,000
The ad says: Amy Acton praised socialist mayor Zohran Mamdani. (Paid for by V-PAC · pro-Ramaswamy super PAC)

4
00:00:08,350 --> 00:00:10,800
The record says: The ad’s own source doesn’t mention her. In the interview, the interviewer said Mamdani’s name. Acton never did.

5
00:00:10,800 --> 00:00:13,000
Verdict: False. Being asked about someone is not the same as praising them.

6
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:16,600
The ad says: His company “charged sick kids $3 million.” (Paid for by Amy Acton campaign)

7
00:00:16,950 --> 00:00:19,400
The record says: It’s Rethymic, a one-time treatment for babies born without a thymus, listed at $2.7 million before insurance.

8
00:00:19,400 --> 00:00:21,600
Verdict: Missing context. The price is real. The villain music is optional.

9
00:00:21,600 --> 00:00:25,200
The ad says: She missed COVID training after tripping on a dog gate. (Paid for by V-PAC · pro-Ramaswamy super PAC)

10
00:00:25,550 --> 00:00:28,000
The record says: She did trip and miss a training. But it was a CDC communications course, months before the pandemic.

11
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:30,200
Verdict: Missing context. The dog gate has been cleared of all charges.

12
00:00:30,200 --> 00:00:33,800
The ad says: Ramaswamy said: “We are making money… that’s what’s most important.” (Paid for by Amy Acton campaign)

13
00:00:34,150 --> 00:00:36,600
The record says: It’s a paraphrase from a 2023 Forbes story recalling a 2011 conversation. Ramaswamy denies saying it.

14
00:00:36,600 --> 00:00:38,800
Verdict: False. Those quotation marks are doing a lot of work.

15
00:00:38,800 --> 00:00:43,200
Ads are built to persuade. The record is built to check. We check every side: knowyourballot.netlify.app. Ohio votes Nov. 3.
